{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering trial of drug decriminalization ends this Sunday, replaced by legislation that reinstates criminal penalties for possessing small quantities of hard substances. Under the new statute, individuals found with fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice between formal charges or entering a treatment protocol. This approach seeks to balance enforcement with health support, though personal possession is now classified as a misdemeanor carrying potential jail time.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law aims to empower police to address public drug use more aggressively while imposing stricter sanctions on sales near sensitive locations like parks. Although it encourages local jurisdictions to establish alternatives to incarceration, it does not force them to do so. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions to their specific resources, whereas critics fear it will result in an inconsistent and unfair system across the state.\n\n***\n\nPolice Chief Bob Day noted that eligibility for diversion programs is quite restrictive, requiring participants to have no other warrants, charges, or history of violence while being medically stable. When officers encounter someone who qualifies, they must contact a dispatch line to connect the individual with a mobile health team. These teams have a strict thirty-minute window to arrive; if they fail to appear within that timeframe, the individual will be taken into custody rather than waiting indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nJust as a person might use their **tongue** to **eat** food after a long day of **shopping**, society must find a way to consume justice without losing its moral compass. The **age** of this new policy marks a significant shift for the **country**, moving away from the previous experimental mod